        "id": 3332404
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-03-27T03:50:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Based on user hints, mark user experience-related I/O as high-priority I/O. Additionally, further filter based on the bio\u0027s bi_flags and bi_size to avoid marking too many I/O as high-priority.",
      "parentUuid": "b839c086_8bc43ae7",
      "revId": "1062c02cb2b5b4ebbfaddff39afcef6879530181",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4ebea616_a3a1ee7f",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 10,
      "author": {
        "id": 1869780
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-03-27T10:53:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Please consider modifying the upstream ioprio cgroup policy (block/blk-ioprio.c) and the Android task_profiles.json file instead of introducing this vendor hook.",
      "parentUuid": "d162d529_60a9845d",
      "revId": "1062c02cb2b5b4ebbfaddff39afcef6879530181",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f9855f97_e3b25e4f",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 10,
      "author": {
        "id": 3332404
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-03-27T12:09:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We have already considered the ioprio cgroup policy, but it still does not meet our needs. We have the following unresolved issues:\n1. For requests of type IOPRIO_CLASS_RT, the `ufshcd_comp_scsi_upiu` function adds the UPIU_CMD_FLAGS_CP flag to the `upiu_flags`, causing UFS to prioritize these types of I/O. However, marking high-priority I/O at the thread granularity can lead to an excessive number of high-priority I/O, which may cause the UFS priority marking to become ineffective.\n2. We want to statistic the distribution of high-priority I/O at different granularities and types in order to set I/O priorities more rationally. We aim to avoid marking too many large-granularity I/O as high-priority I/O, which could result in significant delays for important small-granularity I/O, especially on low-end devices.",
      "parentUuid": "4ebea616_a3a1ee7f",
      "revId": "1062c02cb2b5b4ebbfaddff39afcef6879530181",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4b4e243c_8d51e987",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 10,
      "author": {
        "id": 1869780
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-03-27T16:42:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In case this wouldn\u0027t be clear, `IOPRIO_CLASS_RT` is assigned by the ioprio cgroup policy.\n\nPlease consider implementing the necessary algorithms in the ioprio cgroup policy and configuring the parameters for these algorithms via `task_profiles.json`, either the generic configuration file or the vendor-specific `task_profiles.json` file.\n\nOr alternatively, disable assigning `IOPRIO_CLASS_RT` by overriding this `task_profiles.json` section:\n```\n        {\n          \"Name\": \"SetAttribute\",\n          \"Params\":\n          {\n            \"Name\": \"IoPrioClass\",\n            \"Value\": \"promote-to-rt\",\n            \"Optional\": \"true\"\n          }\n        }\n```",
      "parentUuid": "f9855f97_e3b25e4f",
      "revId": "1062c02cb2b5b4ebbfaddff39afcef6879530181",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4185f665_137912bc",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 10,
      "author": {
        "id": 3332404
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-03-28T10:47:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "1. We expect to monitor the usage of high-priority I/O sent to storage devices through vendor hooks.\n2. The `get_current_ioprio` function sets `bio-\u003ebi_ioprio` based on the `{struct task_struct}-\u003epolicy`, which means that even if the I/O priority cgroup policy is disabled, the I/O priority will still be set. Therefore, it is necessary to monitor the distribution of high-priority I/O across different types and granularities.\n3. Modifying the `task_profiles.json` configuration allows setting I/O priority only at the thread level, and it cannot differentiate between read/write operations or I/O granularity. Excessively marking large-granularity write I/O as high priority can starve important small-granularity read I/O. For lower-end devices, we expect to mark only the read requests and small write requests from foreground applications as high priority in order to optimize application startup times, rather than marking all I/O as high priority. This requirement cannot be met by modifying the `task_profiles.json` configuration.",
      "parentUuid": "4b4e243c_8d51e987",
      "revId": "1062c02cb2b5b4ebbfaddff39afcef6879530181",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    }
  ],
